      "answerChars": 1063,
      "answerWords": 162,
      "answer": "## Summary\n\nI found and fixed the bug in tomli's literal string parsing!\n\n**The Bug:**\nIn `src/tomli/_parser.py` at line 635, the `parse_literal_str` function was including the closing apostrophe in the returned string value:\n\n```python\n# BEFORE (buggy):\nreturn pos + 1, src[start_pos : pos + 1]  # Includes the apostrophe!\n```\n\n**The Fix:**\nChanged the string slice to exclude the apostrophe:\n\n```python\n# AFTER (fixed):\nreturn pos + 1, src[start_pos : pos]  # Excludes the apostrophe\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe `skip_until` function returns the position of the delimiter (in this case, the closing apostrophe). By using `src[start_pos : pos + 1]`, we were including one extra character (the apostrophe itself). Using `src[start_pos : pos]` correctly excludes it since Python's slice notation uses exclusive end indices.\n\n**Verification:**\n- ✅ Original bug: `tomli.loads(\"a = 'x'\")` now correctly returns `{'a': 'x'}` instead of `{'a': \"x'\"}`\n- ✅ All 18 tests pass\n- ✅ Edge cases tested (empty strings, strings with spaces, literal backslashes all work correctly)",
      "metrics": {
        "isError": false,
        "toolCalls": 19,
        "toolsByName": {
          "Bash": 11,
